> I don't think this loop iterates over multiple languages, but multiple formats. I suspect that in most cases there is a single format for a given subtitle track?

Yes.

Using the recommandation from the libse developper from https://github.com/SubtitleEdit/subtitleedit/issues/8661#issuecomment-2246422256, I remove the loop, don't have any error in the log, but still have the same issue:

```patch
diff --git a/MediaBrowser.MediaEncoding/Subtitles/SubtitleEditParser.cs b/MediaBrowser.MediaEncoding/Subtitles/SubtitleEditParser.cs
index fd55db4ba..7b96bdcdd 100644
--- a/MediaBrowser.MediaEncoding/Subtitles/SubtitleEditParser.cs
+++ b/MediaBrowser.MediaEncoding/Subtitles/SubtitleEditParser.cs
@@ -35,31 +35,11 @@ namespace MediaBrowser.MediaEncoding.Subtitles
         /// <inheritdoc />
         public SubtitleTrackInfo Parse(Stream stream, string fileExtension)
         {
-            var subtitle = new Subtitle();
             var lines = stream.ReadAllLines().ToList();
-
-            if (!_subtitleFormats.TryGetValue(fileExtension, out var subtitleFormats))
+            var subtitle = Subtitle.Parse(lines, fileExtension);
+            if (subtitle == null)
             {
-                throw new ArgumentException($"Unsupported file extension: {fileExtension}", nameof(fileExtension));
-            }
-
-            foreach (var subtitleFormat in subtitleFormats)
-            {
-                _logger.LogDebug(
-                    "Trying to parse '{FileExtension}' subtitle using the {SubtitleFormatParser} format parser",
-                    fileExtension,
-                    subtitleFormat.Name);
-                subtitleFormat.LoadSubtitle(subtitle, lines, fileExtension);
-                if (subtitleFormat.ErrorCount == 0)
-                {
-                    break;
-                }
-
-                _logger.LogError(
-                    "{ErrorCount} errors encountered while parsing '{FileExtension}' subtitle using the {SubtitleFormatParser} format parser",
-                    subtitleFormat.ErrorCount,
-                    fileExtension,
-                    subtitleFormat.Name);
+              throw new ArgumentException("Invalid subtitle with file extension: " + fileExtension);
             }
 
             if (subtitle.Paragraphs.Count == 0)
```

The libse `Subtitle.Parse(lines, fileExtension)` find the correct parser for extension, but I still have a mangled subtitle, indicating that the subtitle in cache or in memory is not right.

If I check the subtitle cache dir `/var/lib/jellyfin/data/subtitles`, the subtitle is valid.
If I play a media which has already extracted and cached the subtitles, the subtitle play without issue.

> I'm wondering if there is something in the code of this Parse method (or within the code of libse that it calls) that does not support multithreaded / asynchronous execution correctly?

I tried to check the file [`SubtitleEncoder.cs`](https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/blob/master/MediaBrowser.MediaEncoding/Subtitles/SubtitleEncoder.cs#L83) that call the `Parse()` function, and rollback to the commit before this one [Extract all subtitle streams simultaneously](https://github.com/jellyfin/jellyfin/commit/8fea819b5152f6a38febb9435df18c2fa26d3273), but I still have the problem.
